[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Queries, Mutations, and Subscriptions in GraphQL"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"GraphQL is a query language for your API and a server-side runtime for executing queries by using a type system you define for your data. Unlike REST, which exposes multiple endpoints, GraphQL provides a single endpoint and relies on three core operations: Queries for reading data, Mutations for writing data, and Subscriptions for real-time data streaming."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Queries: Fetching Data"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Queries are used to request data from the server. The defining feature of GraphQL queries is that clients can request exactly what they need and nothing more, avoiding both over-fetching and under-fetching."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"query GetUserProfile {
  user(id: "123") {
    name
    email
    posts {
      title
      createdAt
    }
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Mutations: Modifying Data"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"While queries are strictly for reading data, mutations are used to create, update, or delete data on the server side. Mutations typically return the modified object so the client can immediately update its local state with the new data."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"mutation CreateNewPost {
  createPost(input: { title: "GraphQL Guide", body: "Learning schemas and resolvers" }) {
    id
    title
    status
  }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Subscriptions: Real-Time Updates"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Subscriptions allow the server to push data to connected clients in real-time. They maintain a persistent connection, usually established over WebSockets. When a specific event occurs on the server, the subscribed clients instantly receive the payload."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"subscription OnPostCreated {
  postCreated {
    id
    title
    author {
      name
    }
  }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use operation names (like GetUserProfile) for better debugging, logging, and performance tracking."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage variables in queries and mutations instead of hardcoding arguments into strings."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep subscriptions lightweight since they maintain active server resources over long-lived network connections."}]
